target/

# Cargo.lock IS committed here, deliberately, and the neighbouring
# astra-plugin-sdk/.gitignore is right to do the opposite. The rule is what the
# crate produces, not what it is written in: a library's lockfile binds nobody,
# because the application downstream resolves its own; a binary's lockfile is
# the difference between "built from this tag" and "built from this tag and
# whatever the registry happened to hold that morning".
#
# release-cli.yml builds every leg with --locked and refuses to start without
# this file, so ignoring it made the first release fail in `plan` before any
# job ran. That assertion is the reason the mistake was cheap.
